s and Kubeflow Pipelines retains run completion\n40\tmetadata — \"the data exists; it is just not surfaced\". No code changes are\n41\trequired in either passive component, and no other team is assigned to\n42\tvalidate them (no Impacted Teams table exists; no such assignment anywhere in\n43\tthe strategy). Validation that the reports faithfully reflect the notebook\n44\tcontroller's activity tracking and the KFP run metadata therefore becomes\n45\tacceptance criteria on the Dashboard epic, per the passive-component rule. All\n46\tthree components exist in `.context/architecture-context/`\n47\t(`dashboard.md`, `notebooks.md`, `data-science-pipelines.md`), so no new\n48\tcomponent onboarding chain and no component-existence Investigation is needed.\n49\t\n50\t## Epic List\n51\t\n52\t| ID | Title | Type | Team | Priority |\n53\t|---|---|---|---|---|\n54\t| RHAISTRAT-9016-E001 | Idle and stale resource reports in the web console | Implementation | RHOAI Dashboard | P1 |\n55\t| RHAISTRAT-9016-E002 | Documenting the idle and stale resource reports and safe reclamation | Implementation (docs-authoring) | RHOAI Docs | P2 |\n56\t\n57\t## Dependency DAG\n58\t\n59\t```mermaid\n60\tgraph TD\n61\t    E001[RHAISTRAT-9016-E001: Idle and stale resource reports]\n62\t    E002[RHAISTRAT-9016-E002: Documentation]\n63\t    E001 --> E002\n64\t```\n65\t\n66\t## DAG Justification\n67\t\n68\t| Edge | Rule | Rationale |\n69\t|---|---|---|\n70\t| E001 → E002 | Rule 11 (docs-authoring always last) | Documentation describes the reports that were built; it must reflect the shipped behavior, fields, and configuration. No other Implementation epics exist. |\n71\t\n72\tNo other edges: only one code epic exists (single component, single team, one\n73\tlogical change — a per-project idle/stale resource report surface in the\n74\tconsole), so there are no implementation-to-implementation boundaries to draw.\n75\t\n76\t## HLR Traceability Matrix\n77\t\n78\t| HLR | Priority | Epic | Notes |\n79\t|---|---|---|---|\n80\t| HLR-1 | P1 | RHAISTRAT-9016-E001 | Idle-workbench report (last activity, owner, reserved resources) sourced from notebook-controller activity tracking. |\n81\t| HLR-2 | P1 | RHAISTRAT-9016-E001 | Stale pipeline-run report (completed runs older than a configurable age with retained artifacts) sourced from KFP run metadata. |\n82\t| HLR-3 | P2 | RHAISTRAT-9016-E001 | **bundled**: CSV export of either report is a presentation capability on the same report surfaces and the same data-fetch code as HLR-1/HLR-2 — it shares the UI surfaces and data structures of the P1 work and has no standalone surface of its own, so splitting it out would create a second epic building the same views. The P2 label reads as relative value, not a deferral signal. |\n83\t| HLR-4 | P2 | RHAISTRAT-9016-E002 | Substantive documentation authoring — distinct work stream, separate epic. |\n84\t\n85\tFull coverage: HLR-1 through HLR-4 all map to at least one epic; no orphaned\n86\trequirements. Priority inheritance: E001 is P1 (highest priority of the HLRs it\n87\tenables). E002 is a docs-authoring epic and takes the docs-authoring priority\n88\texception — the strategy frontmatter priority is `Normal`, which maps to P2.\n89\t\n90\t## Epic Boundary Analysis\n91\t\n92\tThe two reports (idle workbenches, stale pipeline runs) are kept in a single\n93\tepic: same component (Dashboard), same team (RHOAI Dashboard), same logical\n94\tchange — the strategy frames them as one capability (\"a single report in the\n95\tRHOAI web console showing idle and stale resources per project\"), they render\n96\ton one console surface, and splitting them would force two epics to build the\n97\tsame report page/section, data-fetch patterns, and per-project navigation\n98\tindependently. The CSV export (HLR-3) stays bundled with them for the same\n99\tsurface-sharing reason (flagged in the traceability matrix above).\n100\t\n101\t## Health Warnings\n102\t\n103\t- **Missing strategy sections**: the strategy has no Impacted Teams table, no\n104\t  Dependencies table, no Acceptance Criteria section, and no Non-Functional\n105\t  Requirements. Team assignments were derived from the architecture-context\n106\t  files (`dashboard.md` → RHOAI Dashboard; docs epic assigned to a\n107\t  documentation team as the docs-authoring work stream implies). Acceptance\n108\t  criteria below were derived from the HLRs and the problem statement.\n109\t- **Scope guard**: the strategy's out-of-scope list excludes automated\n110\t  reclamation/deletion and cost calculations — the reports must surface\n111\t  information only; any UI affordance that deletes/reclaims resources would\n112\t  exceed the strategy's scope.\n113\t- **Data-source exposure**: the strategy asserts the console can source\n114\t  last-activity from the notebook controller and run metadata from Kubeflow\n115\t  Pipelines. The architecture context corroborates that the data is tracked\n116\t  (notebooks.md: last activity tracked per kernel; data-science-pipelines.md:\n117\t  persistence and KFP v2 REST API with runs surfaced in the Dashboard), so\n118\t  this is treated as resolved, not as a genuine unknown — how exactly the\n119\t  console reads each source is an implementation detail for the Dashboard\n120\t  team. If implementation reveals the activity data is not exposed in a form\n121\t  the console can read, that would be a follow-up on the Notebooks component,\n122\t  not part of this decomposition.\n123\t- No priority inversions detected. Epic sizes are within a sprint; no\n124\t  re-split triggers needed.\n125\t\n126\t## Tiered Delivery\n127\t\n128\tNot applicable — no external dependencies with uncertain timing.\n129\t"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":"chatcmpl-tool-8739fbd434aec2eb","agent_id":"a2b5ec70b38551f06","session_id":"05ddb1e2-6b5c-4b16-a274-ad31367aa2ce","uuid":"74b62bb9-5f43-436d-80c4-f92e36dfe2bd","timestamp":"2026-10-23T19:39:12.850Z","tool_result_meta":[{"id":"chatcmpl-tool-899200755b85a1f8","permis